# The changelog and the release notes are append-only lists, and every
# branch appends to the same few groups: two branches each adding a bullet
# conflict on the insertion point, which is a conflict with nothing to
# decide. `union` resolves it by keeping both sides' added lines, in the
# order base-then-ours, and it is built into git — nobody has to configure
# anything for it to apply.
#
# It applies to rebases too, which is where it earns its keep, and it has
# a price worth knowing: these two files then never conflict at all, so
# the same existing entry edited on two branches merges in silence rather
# than being flagged. That is why neither states how many entries it has —
# a count is exactly such an edit, and union would have kept both numbers.
# Section 9 of the organization standard, btclib-org/.github's README.md,
# is where this rule is recorded.
#
# Both lines are in every repository's copy, a tree carrying no
# RELEASE_NOTES.md included: section 2 of that README.md gives the
# release notes to a repository that publishes, and an attribute on a
# path the tree does not hold matches nothing.
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
RELEASE_NOTES.md merge=union

## This repository in particular

# The launchers are what this tree ships, to macOS, Windows and Linux.
# `.bat` is checked out CRLF because cmd.exe reads a batch file line by
# line and a lone LF has cost that reader a label before now, which is
# why `.pre-commit-config.yaml` keeps the line-ending fixer off those
# files and `REVIEWING.md` reads them rather than the diff. `.sh` and
# `.command` are checked out LF because a carriage return is an ordinary
# character where a line ending is expected: `#!/bin/bash` followed by
# one names an interpreter that does not exist, and a `fi` followed by
# one is not `fi`. Neither failure is macOS's in particular, so the
# rule serves every file `git ls-files '*.sh' '*.command'` names rather
# than `macos/`'s alone. `.ps1` is checked out LF too: PowerShell reads
# either, where cmd.exe does not, so there is no failure this tree
# exists to avoid on that side of a mismatch. The `binary` lines name
# what a launcher fetches; nothing tracked matches them today, and the
# day one is committed by mistake git will not try to diff or normalize
# it.
*.exe binary
*.app binary
Bitcoin-Qt binary
run_electrum binary
*.md text
*.sh text eol=lf
*.command text eol=lf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=lf
*.conf text
*.json text

# */checksums.sha256 is the file the updaters write to: macOS's and Linux's
# both reach it through shared/utilities/lib.sh, whose appender writes an LF
# line, and the Windows half appends whatever line ending its writer
# defaults to. Without an eol here git stores whichever one last
# touched the file rather than converging them, so eol=lf normalizes every
# write to the format that shared appender and shasum already produce.
# keys/*.fingerprints is never written by a script, but is the same kind of
# plain text list and gets the same treatment for the same reason -- a
# second unattributed text format in the tree is one more thing to notice
# missing.
*/checksums.sha256 text eol=lf
keys/*.fingerprints text eol=lf
